.TH PDBPARSE 1 2020-04-04 libpdb

.SH NAME
pdbparse \- Display information about a PDB file

.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP 9
.BR pdbparse " [" \-f | \-\-file\-header ]
.br
.RB [ \-S | \-\-section\-headers | \-\-sections ]
.br
.RB [ \-s | \-\-syms | \-\-symbols ]
.br
.RB [ \-\-modules ]
.br
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.RB [ \-v | \-\-version ]
.br
.I pdbfile
.LP

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBpdbparse\fR displays information about a Microsoft Program Database
(PDB) file.
The options control what particular information to display.

\fIpdbfile\fR is the PDB file to be examined.

.SH OPTIONS
The long and short forms of options, shown here as alternatives, are
equivalent. At least one options besides \fB\-h\fR or \fB\-v\fR must be
given.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-file\-header
Display the PDB file header, including information from the PDB stream.
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-section\-headers, \-\-sections
Display information about an executable's sections.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-syms, \-\-symbols
Display all public symbols present in the PDB file, including their name
and relative virtual address (RVA).
Note that all names are in their mangled form.
.TP
.B \-\-modules
Display the DBI module registry by physical module index, including each
module's name, object file path, and symbol entries.
Each symbol entry is shown with its module-stream offset, size, and record
kind.
This option also lists each global procedure reference together with the
record offset where it was found, the target module index, the target symbol
offset, and the target's
.IR section : offset .
If a reference cannot be resolved, the failing record offset is reported and
\fBpdbparse\fR exits with a nonzero status.
This option works on PDB files that lack a PSI, GSI, or global record stream,
and may be combined with
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-s ,
and
.BR \-S .
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show summary of options.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show version of program.
